00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:52.730
So lets start the presentation.

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:57.100
So here we have our own
satellite earth observation,

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:59.430
the satellite constellation.

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:05.730
So we have today in the air
seven satellites, five of them

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:10.560
are optical and two
of them are radar.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.940
So the two main flagships
in this constellation

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.690
are the SPOT satellites and
the Pleiades satellites.

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:24.720
And the two radar satellite that
you see here on the right are

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:30.270
quite well known because we
built the mapping in 3D of all

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.760
the earth, in once we've--

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:35.240
those interferometric engines.

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:39.300
So it provides the best and
more accurate 3D mapping

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:42.450
of the earth.

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:45.570
So lets start from
a long time ago.

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:51.990
So 30 years ago, we launched
our first satellites,

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.000
which was Spot One.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.640
And it was the first
civilian satellites

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:58.850
with the telescopic
capabilities.

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:01.380
so enabling the 3D capacity.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:05.750
So since all of our
satellites are able to provide

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:08.190
the 3D processing
of the earth, which

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:15.360
is quite important nowadays
because 3D is a key feature.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:19.800
So 30 years ago we were already
digital, as you can see.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:23.610
Some of you may
remember this time,

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:26.340
but most of the
people in this room

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:30.060
are younger than 30 years old,
so they have not noticed that.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:33.150
You may see that we were
already fashioned by the time,

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:34.700
wearing a beard.

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:42.840
And we were doing our best
to satisfy our customer

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:48.450
and having a map of the earth
and planning the satellite's

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:49.890
shape on the earth.

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:54.330
So that was quite
challenging by the time.

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:56.950
We were not yet object storage.

00:04:56.950 --> 00:04:59.700
So putting an image
in the object storage

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.890
was not as efficient.

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:04.620
So here it was tape.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.900
So we had to remove
the tape to put back

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:08.730
the tape to get the image, etc.

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:13.920
So it was interesting, but
everything was digital.

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:17.430
And at this time we
were very proud of that.

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:21.330
Our main competitor by that
time was a Russian company,

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:24.510
a Russian satellite, and
they were analog satellite.

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:29.370
So it was-- we were
very proud of that.

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:33.090
It was taking days
and weeks and weeks

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:35.940
to get an image,
and even the image,

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:40.290
the sensor were digital but
the key issue by that time

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:46.380
was to get to the
customers a good printing

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:50.340
shot of those images
on a very strong paper.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:53.400
Because most of our customers
were not digital by the time,

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:55.080
they were analog,
and those images

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:58.290
where we are pushed on
their analog stereoplotter

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:01.840
in order to do their mapping.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:02.340
OK.

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:06.000
We improved days,
years after years,

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.490
and now we are providing
in a digital form

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:12.610
to our customer digital
imagery, nice ones.

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:16.710
We have nice colors,
as you can see.

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:21.510
It was hours, I
say in the past it

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:23.140
was because now we
are on the Cloud

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:25.230
so we are not hours anymore.

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:28.680
But it's still the same images.

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:31.590
OK.

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:34.530
So we move to the
Cloud, and I will

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:38.910
describe what it means for
us to move in the cloud,

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:43.650
to the cloud, but for us
it has been a major step

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:45.490
to work efficiently.

00:06:45.490 --> 00:06:48.570
So here we have--

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:50.040
now we are able--

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:53.120
now whole of our
images on the cloud.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:57.210
And we are able to
access them within a time

00:06:57.210 --> 00:07:02.580
frame of half a second.

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:07.380
We can have unlimited
users, the platform

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:11.980
can host an unlimited
number of images.

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:13.710
It's worldwide.

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:20.130
This morning Eric Schmidt
said that the good thing

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:24.740
with the Google Network is
that you can access the data

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:25.740
everywhere in the world.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:28.410
And it's really true
it's really true for us.

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:32.490
We are using this
functionality and our customers

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:34.700
are very well served.

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:39.240
Today we are storing
a part of our images,

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:42.980
we are not at a full power
because we are looking,

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:45.880
we are waiting a little bit to
see how things are going on.

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:50.430
So it's off of our capacities,
of our satellite capacities.

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:57.180
And it represent half a
petabyte per year, roughly.

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:01.740
We are currently
producing, developing,

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:05.100
building our next
constellation satellites,

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:09.550
which will be far more
accurate, far more precise.

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:12.810
We have a far more--
of a larger efficiency.

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:18.300
And we will be at a resume
of two petabyte a year.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:23.130
So our satellites are
fully, directly connected

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:25.870
to the cloud, and we
are feeding the cloud

00:08:25.870 --> 00:08:32.200
at a resume of 2 million of
square kilometers per week.

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:36.480
So it's represented at
roughly four times the surface

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:38.060
of Spain.

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:39.780
per week.

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:42.210
So few words about the process.

00:08:42.210 --> 00:08:44.480
So before deciding
to go to the cloud,

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:48.370
we had to pick the
right client for us.

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:51.270
So we went through an RFP.

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:55.110
So we are in an Airbus company.

00:08:55.110 --> 00:08:57.270
I mean, you have
to go such things.

00:08:57.270 --> 00:09:01.140
And so it was seven
the cloud providers

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.150
provided their feedback.

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:09.510
And obviously we pick
Google for the many reasons.

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:11.420
I could highlight some of them.

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:15.120
First one there was a
commitment of Google

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:21.320
that they will support us
bringing to life our system

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:22.820
and being successful.

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:23.640
They did it.

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:26.700
I have to admit that
they have been very, very

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:28.390
efficient doing that.

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:31.590
And the best is that we
are presenting together

00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:32.750
with Matthieu.

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:36.540
Matthieu help us a lot
during this process.

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:39.300
Another point is security.

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:41.400
As we have all
heard this morning

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:45.330
with HSBC and the others
going to the cloud security

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:46.590
is a major issue.

00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:53.610
So Google do have the right
features for this, for us.

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:56.090
And other point is
about the roadmap.

00:09:56.090 --> 00:09:57.720
In their roadmap
there was-- we were

00:09:57.720 --> 00:10:01.260
very interested by a
high machine learning.

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:05.340
So it was already disclosed
when we sign or contract,

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:08.790
because for us a high machine
learning is very crucial

00:10:08.790 --> 00:10:12.120
in order to extract
automatically

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:15.570
information from our images.

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:23.160
So we pick Google and we
start implementing our system

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:25.260
on the cloud platform.

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:31.980
So the first step was about
to connect our satellites

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:33.900
to the cloud.

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:36.630
So we have been
around the Earth,

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:39.150
and we have our satellites
flying over them.

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:43.830
And when they have images,
they are just uploading,

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:45.730
downloading those images.

00:10:45.730 --> 00:10:49.350
And we develop internally
all the connections in order

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:53.280
to have a fully automatic
process from the satellite

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:57.780
up to where we call it
the Intelligence Digital

00:10:57.780 --> 00:11:00.720
Platform on the cloud Platform.

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:07.320
So we uploaded all of our
images on the object storage.

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:09.780
And when we do that
at the same time

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:11.790
we need to do some
processing in order

00:11:11.790 --> 00:11:16.170
to prepare them to be easily
accessible and rapidly

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:20.430
accessible to J reference
them, to adjust them in order

00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:22.330
to be fully cartographic.

00:11:22.330 --> 00:11:27.450
So this is done in the
cloud infrastructure.

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:31.450
Then last year, end of
September last year,

00:11:31.450 --> 00:11:35.710
based on the platform, we
opened our first service,

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:37.140
which is One Atlas.

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:41.230
So you are welcome to
subscribe to it for a fee,

00:11:41.230 --> 00:11:42.460
it's not it's not free.

00:11:42.460 --> 00:11:46.540
But we open the first
service, so it gives access

00:11:46.540 --> 00:11:50.830
to our catalog of
data and images,

00:11:50.830 --> 00:11:54.040
which are just sitting
there, to be used.

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:56.950
I will provide you
a real time demo

00:11:56.950 --> 00:11:59.340
just right after this slide.

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:01.990
And we are working
and we have already

00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:07.210
developed new
services, solution,

00:12:07.210 --> 00:12:12.560
fully developed native cloud.

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:16.690
So we went through the
process that Eric Schmidt

00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:17.740
described this morning.

00:12:17.740 --> 00:12:20.830
I mean, the first [INAUDIBLE]
steps is you can take your code

00:12:20.830 --> 00:12:24.450
and you put your code, you are
30 years old code or whatever,

00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:27.190
and you put your code
as it is on the VM.

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:28.850
You can do that.

00:12:28.850 --> 00:12:30.850
But when you are
doing that, especially

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:33.540
for a remote sensing
image processing,

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:38.710
you are not using the best of
the elasticity and capacity

00:12:38.710 --> 00:12:39.320
of the cloud.

00:12:39.320 --> 00:12:45.850
So we had to redevelop not
most but part of our processing

00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:49.690
in order to have a [INAUDIBLE]
less processings, processes,

00:12:49.690 --> 00:12:55.090
and in order to use fully
the elasticity of the cloud.

00:12:55.090 --> 00:12:58.620
So we have developed that,
and we have a few solution.

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:03.630
And I will introduce, just
after the first walk through,

00:13:03.630 --> 00:13:06.090
the workflow that we
implemented in order

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:10.530
to do a very
high-end processing.

00:13:10.530 --> 00:13:15.720
So certainly also
on the analytics

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:18.480
we are working now on AI.

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:20.490
As it has been
introduced this morning,

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:22.410
we are using a TensorFlow.

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:27.180
So we put it by ourself
or we are using now ML.

00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:30.960
And we have the ability
today automatically

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:34.280
to extract all the
clouds which are

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:36.550
in the images, the
planes which are

00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:39.490
in the images, a
key subject for us.

00:13:39.490 --> 00:13:45.480
And we are starting, I
mean, a few other subjects

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:48.620
which are quite confidential
for the time being.

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:59.140
Few words about how
an industrial company

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:06.220
such as Airbus, the
difficulty to go to the cloud,

00:14:06.220 --> 00:14:07.570
to the public cloud.

00:14:07.570 --> 00:14:09.890
And similar that in 3 point.

00:14:09.890 --> 00:14:13.870
So security, we already
are ready to describe

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:15.460
that a little bit.

00:14:15.460 --> 00:14:19.420
So this morning HSBC, say
I was quite impressed.

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:21.820
Because they say
that in six months

00:14:21.820 --> 00:14:23.830
they were on the public cloud.

00:14:23.830 --> 00:14:27.820
And, in fact, for us it
took us six months just

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:32.530
to do the paperwork and to get
the stamp and the clearance

00:14:32.530 --> 00:14:40.450
from our security team
because it's our first attempt

00:14:40.450 --> 00:14:41.960
to go to the public cloud.

00:14:41.960 --> 00:14:44.680
So now it will be
faster, certainly.

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:50.140
So this process basically
took us around one year.

00:14:50.140 --> 00:14:53.860
So another key point for us
a standard industry company

00:14:53.860 --> 00:14:55.610
is the cloud technologies.

00:14:55.610 --> 00:15:00.150
So we have very talented
engineer in the company.

00:15:00.150 --> 00:15:04.480
They are able to fly
rockets, to build satellites,

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:06.760
to create optics, whatever.

00:15:06.760 --> 00:15:10.090
But the technology
are not yet there.

00:15:10.090 --> 00:15:14.190
And it can go very fast,
but it's not sitting there.

00:15:14.190 --> 00:15:17.900
So we have to go
step by step in order

00:15:17.900 --> 00:15:22.000
to increase the team which
do have the knowledge.

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:25.300
And also it's so fast.

00:15:25.300 --> 00:15:27.014
And that can be an
issue, and we don't

00:15:27.014 --> 00:15:28.930
know how we will deal
with that in the future.

00:15:28.930 --> 00:15:31.600
But it's so fast
that as soon as you

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:35.066
have done something in
a way it's already old

00:15:35.066 --> 00:15:36.190
and there is something new.

00:15:36.190 --> 00:15:38.780
And so what I will
describe today

00:15:38.780 --> 00:15:40.900
it's something that
we did six months ago,

00:15:40.900 --> 00:15:43.390
but we are already working
on the next step, which

00:15:43.390 --> 00:15:46.330
is using a queue when it
has the contenders and not

00:15:46.330 --> 00:15:47.880
the VM anymore.

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:50.990
So we are already changing
our way to do things.

00:15:50.990 --> 00:15:56.230
So it's kind of a difficulty
for an industrial company.

00:15:56.230 --> 00:16:00.670
And the other point is our
goal is to be in operation.

00:16:00.670 --> 00:16:05.830
And so I don't know who said
that this morning, but it's

00:16:05.830 --> 00:16:09.220
very, very difficult for
a company like us which

00:16:09.220 --> 00:16:14.050
is used to do something
