00:30:10.570 --> 00:30:13.355
MATTHIEU MAYRAN: Thanks.

00:30:13.355 --> 00:30:17.100
So I'm going to start by
explaining a few Google Cloud

00:30:17.100 --> 00:30:21.590
Platform concepts and to be able
to actually scale instances.

00:30:21.590 --> 00:30:23.250
So the first
concept that we have

00:30:23.250 --> 00:30:25.240
are actually regions and zones.

00:30:25.240 --> 00:30:27.540
So we have data centers,
Google data centers,

00:30:27.540 --> 00:30:29.610
in different locations
in the world,

00:30:29.610 --> 00:30:30.750
and we call them regions.

00:30:30.750 --> 00:30:32.490
So you can see
here some of them.

00:30:32.490 --> 00:30:36.250
We are working on expanding
them during this year.

00:30:36.250 --> 00:30:39.960
The region is usually there are
several regions in a continent,

00:30:39.960 --> 00:30:43.200
and then within a region you
will have at least two zones

00:30:43.200 --> 00:30:47.895
but even more to set up things
such as like high availability,

00:30:47.895 --> 00:30:50.940
load overflowing, etc.

00:30:50.940 --> 00:30:57.450
So in the zone, what you can
do is actually run some task,

00:30:57.450 --> 00:30:59.160
but you need to
understand what sort

00:30:59.160 --> 00:31:01.440
of task you need to
run and do these tasks

00:31:01.440 --> 00:31:02.830
have right usage patterns.

00:31:02.830 --> 00:31:05.600
So if you look at
use patterns there

00:31:05.600 --> 00:31:06.960
are like two most common ones.

00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:09.960
One is about its unpredictable.

00:31:09.960 --> 00:31:12.630
So if you actually run,
let's say, a website.

00:31:12.630 --> 00:31:15.690
So for example, when you
create a website to show images

00:31:15.690 --> 00:31:19.590
of earth or if you run a website
for like ticketing system

00:31:19.590 --> 00:31:21.420
you might actually
have a bunch of users

00:31:21.420 --> 00:31:23.500
that actually you arrive at
the same time on the website,

00:31:23.500 --> 00:31:25.240
and you need to scale
like in a second.

00:31:25.240 --> 00:31:28.180
You might be like 100,000
users or something like that,

00:31:28.180 --> 00:31:29.730
and you cannot
predict that, right?

00:31:29.730 --> 00:31:32.840
So this is usually when
you need an auto scaler.

00:31:32.840 --> 00:31:35.730
It's to actually be
able to scale based

00:31:35.730 --> 00:31:39.510
on that unpredictable load.

00:31:39.510 --> 00:31:41.430
The second one is a
bit more predictable.

00:31:41.430 --> 00:31:45.430
It's when you're on a website,
let's say around the world,

00:31:45.430 --> 00:31:48.720
and then the US wake
up, Europe and Asia

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:50.110
go to bed, something like that.

00:31:50.110 --> 00:31:53.920
So if you look your over a
week a month or like a few days

00:31:53.920 --> 00:31:56.080
you actually see
waves of traffic.

00:31:56.080 --> 00:31:58.600
And this is predictable,
but it's also something

00:31:58.600 --> 00:32:00.220
you need to be able to scale.

00:32:00.220 --> 00:32:04.060
So in both cases you have to
be able to scale up and down.

00:32:04.060 --> 00:32:06.250
The reason is if you
over provision you're

00:32:06.250 --> 00:32:08.800
going to spend a lot of money
that you don't want to spend,

00:32:08.800 --> 00:32:11.377
and if you under
provision, usually

00:32:11.377 --> 00:32:13.210
you have to have
[INAUDIBLE] on your website

00:32:13.210 --> 00:32:14.180
or your applications.

00:32:14.180 --> 00:32:15.679
So if you don't
have enough machines

00:32:15.679 --> 00:32:19.150
it's probably going to be
down for a lot of the users.

00:32:19.150 --> 00:32:21.190
So I think we made the
point that we actually

00:32:21.190 --> 00:32:22.750
need auto scaler,
but you also need

00:32:22.750 --> 00:32:25.400
to understand what sort of
auto scaler you want to run.

00:32:25.400 --> 00:32:29.740
So if we look at tasks, you
can have like short tasks,

00:32:29.740 --> 00:32:32.950
for example serving of
websites or creating

00:32:32.950 --> 00:32:34.320
thumbnails for images.

00:32:34.320 --> 00:32:36.850
So usually that takes
about like milliseconds.

00:32:36.850 --> 00:32:38.560
Yeah, usually milliseconds.

00:32:38.560 --> 00:32:41.050
Or you can have
actually long processing

00:32:41.050 --> 00:32:45.580
tasks such as transcoding
videos or rendering or actually

00:32:45.580 --> 00:32:47.620
processing image
satellite, for example.

00:32:47.620 --> 00:32:49.600
And those ones work differently.

00:32:49.600 --> 00:32:52.300
And this is when you're
going to define, OK,

00:32:52.300 --> 00:32:53.650
how do I go to scale these?

00:32:53.650 --> 00:32:56.170
So this is what we're
going to look at,

00:32:56.170 --> 00:32:59.350
but first we need to understand
the core component of running

00:32:59.350 --> 00:33:02.140
a task, which is an instance.

00:33:02.140 --> 00:33:05.170
This is the basics
of compute engine.

00:33:05.170 --> 00:33:07.420
So with our instances
you can install, like,

00:33:07.420 --> 00:33:09.370
operating systems,
start up screens.

00:33:09.370 --> 00:33:11.170
You attach them to
a network firewall,

00:33:11.170 --> 00:33:14.170
so a lot of the sessions here
are going to explain that.

00:33:14.170 --> 00:33:17.320
But one of the specificity
of compute engine

00:33:17.320 --> 00:33:19.930
is that you can actually
create custom VMs.

00:33:19.930 --> 00:33:22.540
So if you're not happy with
the amount of CPU and memory

00:33:22.540 --> 00:33:24.910
that we give you as a
set, you can actually

00:33:24.910 --> 00:33:26.500
increase or decrease
some of them

00:33:26.500 --> 00:33:28.570
to create your own machine.

00:33:28.570 --> 00:33:30.520
And this is useful
because you can actually

00:33:30.520 --> 00:33:33.610
fit to the performance
that you think you need,

00:33:33.610 --> 00:33:38.700
or you can fit to the price
that your CFO thinks you need.

00:33:38.700 --> 00:33:41.920
If you get it wrong though we
can actually give you an advice

00:33:41.920 --> 00:33:42.440
as well.

00:33:42.440 --> 00:33:44.170
So we look [INAUDIBLE]
all the time

00:33:44.170 --> 00:33:47.170
using our own internal tools
like machine running, most

00:33:47.170 --> 00:33:48.340
likely.

00:33:48.340 --> 00:33:50.770
We can tell you, all oh, I
think here you might actually

00:33:50.770 --> 00:33:53.407
want to downgrade
what you've set up

00:33:53.407 --> 00:33:55.240
because you're spending
too much money based

00:33:55.240 --> 00:33:56.730
on what you actually need.

00:33:56.730 --> 00:33:59.080
Or we can also say,
look, you actually

00:33:59.080 --> 00:34:01.210
should spend more
money and increase

00:34:01.210 --> 00:34:04.360
what you're using to
improve the performance.

00:34:04.360 --> 00:34:07.330
This is usually what we
call vertical scaling.

00:34:07.330 --> 00:34:11.100
So you have an instance and
you just make it stronger

00:34:11.100 --> 00:34:13.510
or you make it less strong.

00:34:13.510 --> 00:34:15.489
But it's not always
like the best case,

00:34:15.489 --> 00:34:18.219
because if you actually want
to process like gigabytes

00:34:18.219 --> 00:34:21.460
of images, or if you want to
actually serve one million user

00:34:21.460 --> 00:34:23.110
you can go with
one huge computer

00:34:23.110 --> 00:34:26.050
but there's only so much CPU
and memory that you can actually

00:34:26.050 --> 00:34:28.300
put in a computer
all in an instance.

00:34:28.300 --> 00:34:31.810
So one useful thing
to do is actually

00:34:31.810 --> 00:34:34.510
do always auto scaling,
which is multiplying

00:34:34.510 --> 00:34:36.889
the amount of computers
that you're using,

00:34:36.889 --> 00:34:38.783
which works for a
lot of use cases.

00:34:38.783 --> 00:34:40.449
Doesn't work all the
time, sometimes you

00:34:40.449 --> 00:34:41.560
need a massive computer.

00:34:41.560 --> 00:34:44.320
But more and more
always auto scaling

00:34:44.320 --> 00:34:46.179
is becoming the standard.

00:34:46.179 --> 00:34:48.520
So to do that in
compute engine you use

00:34:48.520 --> 00:34:51.159
managed instance group,
which is leveraging

00:34:51.159 --> 00:34:54.429
an instance template,
[INAUDIBLE] holes here.

00:34:54.429 --> 00:34:57.130
So the template you can
create it in different ways.

00:34:57.130 --> 00:34:59.320
You can actually set
it up yourself saying,

00:34:59.320 --> 00:35:01.200
like you did for
an instant, I want

00:35:01.200 --> 00:35:03.100
that much CPU, that
much memory, and this

00:35:03.100 --> 00:35:05.620
is going to be my template
that I'm going to reuse.

00:35:05.620 --> 00:35:08.950
Or you can actually create
or reuse an instance

00:35:08.950 --> 00:35:11.590
that you already had
and create an image

00:35:11.590 --> 00:35:15.040
from it and from that
image creates a template.

00:35:15.040 --> 00:35:18.050
And another solution we have
is actually to use containers.

00:35:18.050 --> 00:35:20.410
So if you actually set
up containers and, like,

00:35:20.410 --> 00:35:23.710
build it in Docker for example
and push it to our registry,

00:35:23.710 --> 00:35:25.992
you can use that container
to create a template.

00:35:25.992 --> 00:35:28.450
So a lot of different options
it depends on what you prefer

00:35:28.450 --> 00:35:29.620
and how you want to work.

00:35:29.620 --> 00:35:32.770
The thing is now you've got a
template so you can actually

00:35:32.770 --> 00:35:34.600
create an instance group.

00:35:34.600 --> 00:35:37.450
And that instance
group can be regional,

00:35:37.450 --> 00:35:39.250
for example like
across different zones.

00:35:39.250 --> 00:35:40.896
So if you need a
high availability,

00:35:40.896 --> 00:35:42.270
or it can be on
one zone as well.

00:35:42.270 --> 00:35:43.990
In that case it's two zones.

00:35:43.990 --> 00:35:45.140
And it will be low balance.

00:35:45.140 --> 00:35:48.070
So in front of it you would
be able to put a low balancer.

00:35:48.070 --> 00:35:50.680
We're going to look at
that on the next slides.

00:35:50.680 --> 00:35:53.770
Which help balancing
the load, thus the name.

00:35:53.770 --> 00:35:56.500
And you're able to auto scale
it up and down depending

00:35:56.500 --> 00:35:58.060
on your need, but
you would also be

00:35:58.060 --> 00:35:59.530
able to do some rolling updates.

00:35:59.530 --> 00:36:02.020
So if some days you're
like, OK, I need a new card

00:36:02.020 --> 00:36:05.410
or I need like a stronger
instance or something,

00:36:05.410 --> 00:36:08.200
you will be able to
automatically update

00:36:08.200 --> 00:36:09.900
the instance group.

00:36:09.900 --> 00:36:12.190
You would also be able
to auto healing instance.

00:36:12.190 --> 00:36:14.170
So if one is actually
dying, you're

00:36:14.170 --> 00:36:17.050
able to replace it automatically
by another one using

00:36:17.050 --> 00:36:19.010
the template.

00:36:19.010 --> 00:36:21.700
So let's go back to one of the
concept, the load balancer.

00:36:21.700 --> 00:36:23.860
So we have different
load balancers

00:36:23.860 --> 00:36:25.570
that we offer as a service.

00:36:25.570 --> 00:36:32.060
We have like HTTP, HTTPS, TCB
proxy, SSL/TSL proxy, or UDP.

00:36:32.060 --> 00:36:34.660
So different level on
the networking stack.

00:36:34.660 --> 00:36:37.480
This one, for example,
is an HTTP load balancer,

00:36:37.480 --> 00:36:40.540
which means like in that
case you will have an HTTP.

00:36:40.540 --> 00:36:43.390
So you get one global
unique IP worldwide

00:36:43.390 --> 00:36:46.600
that you can plug your
domain into, for example.

00:36:46.600 --> 00:36:50.170
And behind the scene it will
connect to backend services

00:36:50.170 --> 00:36:51.940
with a bunch of little steps.

00:36:51.940 --> 00:36:54.400
And the backend services will
be able to actually route

00:36:54.400 --> 00:36:59.680
a request to the right instance
in a right [INAUDIBLE] zone.

00:36:59.680 --> 00:37:01.930
If you have different zone
or even in the right region

00:37:01.930 --> 00:37:04.780
you can do session affinities
and a lot of things like that.

00:37:07.350 --> 00:37:09.970
On the features of the
manage instance group,

00:37:09.970 --> 00:37:10.930
so we saw some of them.

00:37:10.930 --> 00:37:12.460
You have like
rolling update, which

00:37:12.460 --> 00:37:17.140
will apply a template
to a number of instances

00:37:17.140 --> 00:37:17.900
at the same time.

00:37:17.900 --> 00:37:19.510
So you can define
how many instances,

00:37:19.510 --> 00:37:21.250
but you're also
able to pause if you

00:37:21.250 --> 00:37:22.960
need to double
check a few things,

00:37:22.960 --> 00:37:24.640
and even roll back
if you decided

00:37:24.640 --> 00:37:26.440
that you made a mistake.

00:37:26.440 --> 00:37:29.260
We do that sometimes in Google.

00:37:29.260 --> 00:37:31.600
Then you also have
the autohealing,

00:37:31.600 --> 00:37:34.410
as we saw earlier, which is not
the same as the load balancer.

00:37:34.410 --> 00:37:36.600
So it's leveraging
on the healthcheck,

00:37:36.600 --> 00:37:39.590
and the healthcheck
appears in two things.

00:37:39.590 --> 00:37:40.830
One is for the load balancer.

00:37:40.830 --> 00:37:42.940
So a load balancer
uses that check.

00:37:42.940 --> 00:37:45.520
OK, is that instance working?

00:37:45.520 --> 00:37:47.560
If it is, it's fine I
can load traffic to it.

00:37:47.560 --> 00:37:50.770
If not, I will just
use another one.

00:37:50.770 --> 00:37:53.170
But the autohealing
use another one,

00:37:53.170 --> 00:37:55.990
which is OK that
instance is not fine

00:37:55.990 --> 00:37:57.810
and it's kind of not doing much.

00:37:57.810 --> 00:38:00.160
Then I would just like
kill that instance

00:38:00.160 --> 00:38:02.300
and replace it by another one.

00:38:02.300 --> 00:38:05.290
And again it's using
the group template.

00:38:05.290 --> 00:38:07.930
And the last one is the auto
scaler that you can put do it.

00:38:07.930 --> 00:38:09.990
So you can actually
use different metrics.

00:38:09.990 --> 00:38:13.220
It can be based on CPU, it can
be based on the load balancing

00:38:13.220 --> 00:38:15.460
load, it can be based
on custom metrics

00:38:15.460 --> 00:38:17.080
that you have in the monitoring.

00:38:17.080 --> 00:38:18.960
So you can create your own.

00:38:18.960 --> 00:38:21.607
It can be queue based, which
we are going to look later,

00:38:21.607 --> 00:38:23.440
or it can get a combination
of some of them,

00:38:23.440 --> 00:38:26.620
depending on what you need.

00:38:26.620 --> 00:38:32.010
The auto scaler, just to explain
a little bit on how it works.

00:38:32.010 --> 00:38:34.860
First, time wise
it will actually

00:38:34.860 --> 00:38:40.020
look at how your instances
are behaving across like a one

00:38:40.020 --> 00:38:41.050
minute sliding window.

00:38:41.050 --> 00:38:44.400
So it will look at like across
the whole fleet of instance

00:38:44.400 --> 00:38:48.030
in your instance group,
OK, how you doing CPU wise

00:38:48.030 --> 00:38:50.190
or how you doing
metric wise basically.

00:38:50.190 --> 00:38:51.630
And then every 10
seconds it would

00:38:51.630 --> 00:38:53.230
be able to say, OK,
I need to scale up

00:38:53.230 --> 00:38:55.260
or I need to scale
down based on that.

00:38:55.260 --> 00:38:57.210
And usually it takes
about 30 seconds

00:38:57.210 --> 00:38:59.830
to actually have a
VM up and running.

00:38:59.830 --> 00:39:01.680
It's also cross zones,
so if you actually

00:39:01.680 --> 00:39:03.780
have an instance group
that is regional,

00:39:03.780 --> 00:39:06.310
you're going to be
able to create--

00:39:06.310 --> 00:39:09.770
well, the auto scaler would be
able to create instances itself

00:39:09.770 --> 00:39:14.070
and rebalance the work and
failover automatically.

00:39:14.070 --> 00:39:16.260
And the last one is
money because everyone

00:39:16.260 --> 00:39:20.160
has a CFO / so we
try to fit it as

00:39:20.160 --> 00:39:22.530
much as we can to like
the minimum of instances

00:39:22.530 --> 00:39:23.620
that you need.

00:39:23.620 --> 00:39:25.830
We also enable you to
scale down to zero.

00:39:25.830 --> 00:39:28.620
So if you don't need
to process anything

00:39:28.620 --> 00:39:30.450
you don't need an instance.

00:39:30.450 --> 00:39:32.580
And it's also leveraging
the permanent pricing

00:39:32.580 --> 00:39:35.820
that we have in Google, which
means if you are running

00:39:35.820 --> 00:39:37.272
an instance for
17 minutes you're

00:39:37.272 --> 00:39:39.480
going to be charged for 17
minutes and not a full two

00:39:39.480 --> 00:39:42.370
hours.

00:39:42.370 --> 00:39:43.990
So I'm going to do
a little demo now,

00:39:43.990 --> 00:39:46.360
hopefully it's going to work.

00:39:46.360 --> 00:39:50.146
And it's showing how to
resize images on the fly.

00:39:50.146 --> 00:39:51.520
So if you're in
[INAUDIBLE] tech,

00:39:51.520 --> 00:39:55.120
for example or in
certain verticals,

00:39:55.120 --> 00:39:57.580
you usually have like
maybe a big image somewhere

00:39:57.580 --> 00:39:59.617
and you don't want to
create every single image

00:39:59.617 --> 00:40:01.450
for every single phone,
every single tablet,

00:40:01.450 --> 00:40:04.120
every single desktop,
and all that.

00:40:04.120 --> 00:40:06.910
So sometimes it's actually
fast enough to actually

00:40:06.910 --> 00:40:09.230
just resize it on the fly.

00:40:09.230 --> 00:40:12.500
So I'm going to have my
