It's actually using
several things.

00:44:03.620 --> 00:44:08.200
The first one is Nginx, which
has actually an image filtering

00:44:08.200 --> 00:44:09.200
that you can install.

00:44:09.200 --> 00:44:11.541
So this is more or less
a default configuration

00:44:11.541 --> 00:44:12.415
that I change myself.

00:44:15.160 --> 00:44:16.860
I put that set up
into a container.

00:44:16.860 --> 00:44:19.420
So this is a Dockerfile
to create a container,

00:44:19.420 --> 00:44:23.770
and then you can build it and
apply it to two hours registry

00:44:23.770 --> 00:44:27.770
and leverage that registry
to do several things.

00:44:27.770 --> 00:44:30.500
The first one is to actually
create our instance template.

00:44:30.500 --> 00:44:33.010
And you can see it
takes like a few lines.

00:44:33.010 --> 00:44:36.910
So I just opened the
firewall for HTTP server,

00:44:36.910 --> 00:44:39.280
which means we can actually
HTTP request the theme.

00:44:39.280 --> 00:44:41.260
I used the micro
instance so it's small

00:44:41.260 --> 00:44:44.350
so I can show you the scaling.

00:44:44.350 --> 00:44:46.780
Then from that template, you
can create an instance group.

00:44:46.780 --> 00:44:48.350
From that instance
group you say,

00:44:48.350 --> 00:44:50.110
OK, I want to use
the template for it

00:44:50.110 --> 00:44:54.100
and I want one
instance in there.

00:44:54.100 --> 00:44:55.660
And I make it
regional, which means

00:44:55.660 --> 00:44:59.500
I'm going to get automatically
actually higher availability

00:44:59.500 --> 00:45:00.820
and fadeover.

00:45:00.820 --> 00:45:02.570
And then that instance
group I will set up

00:45:02.570 --> 00:45:05.290
an auto scaler, which is going
to be based on one instance

00:45:05.290 --> 00:45:08.900
at a maximum of 20, because
you have four again.

00:45:08.900 --> 00:45:11.410
And then I want
it to kick in when

00:45:11.410 --> 00:45:13.720
I have like an overall
usage of my instance

00:45:13.720 --> 00:45:16.660
group of 60% of CPU.

00:45:16.660 --> 00:45:18.580
And then I give you
the cool down panel

00:45:18.580 --> 00:45:21.490
of 90 second, which means like
for instances that I actually

00:45:21.490 --> 00:45:24.580
started I'm just going
to say for a minute 30

00:45:24.580 --> 00:45:27.970
they should then be taken
into consideration in my auto

00:45:27.970 --> 00:45:30.422
scaling decisions.

00:45:30.422 --> 00:45:31.880
So this is more or
less what I have

00:45:31.880 --> 00:45:35.910
to do to create the
demo that I showed you.

00:45:38.420 --> 00:45:40.130
So that works fine
because, as you saw,

00:45:40.130 --> 00:45:42.290
it takes a millisecond
to resize that image.

00:45:42.290 --> 00:45:44.900
But in our best use
case when you actually

00:45:44.900 --> 00:45:46.640
have like huge
satellite images that

00:45:46.640 --> 00:45:50.459
can take 10 minutes up to like
a few hours to get processed,

00:45:50.459 --> 00:45:52.250
you actually don't want
to kill an instance

00:45:52.250 --> 00:45:56.300
when it's almost done processing
an image after a few hours.

00:45:56.300 --> 00:45:58.100
And you're like, OK,
yeah well actually we

00:45:58.100 --> 00:46:00.619
don't need you anymore. and
you would have to reprocess.

00:46:00.619 --> 00:46:02.660
So there's a few things
that you can put in place

00:46:02.660 --> 00:46:05.010
to actually prevent that.

00:46:05.010 --> 00:46:08.680
And one of them is to use
a Pub/Sub scale, Pub/Sub

00:46:08.680 --> 00:46:10.640
based autoscaler.

00:46:10.640 --> 00:46:13.920
Which in that case you will
have on the left hand side,

00:46:13.920 --> 00:46:16.670
you will put an
image automatically

00:46:16.670 --> 00:46:18.140
an App Engine or
cloud function is

00:46:18.140 --> 00:46:20.210
going to look at, oh,
there's a new image.

00:46:20.210 --> 00:46:23.000
Please change that
and process it.

00:46:23.000 --> 00:46:25.370
So it will put that
in Cloud Pub-Sub.

00:46:25.370 --> 00:46:27.770
And then a set of work that
you can see on the bottom

00:46:27.770 --> 00:46:29.780
is going to be auto
scaled, and we'll just--

00:46:29.780 --> 00:46:31.789
one worker will just pick
any match processing,

00:46:31.789 --> 00:46:33.830
do whatever all they have
to do, and then save it

00:46:33.830 --> 00:46:36.350
back to Cloud Storage.

00:46:36.350 --> 00:46:40.010
To prevent instances to be
killed while they're actually

00:46:40.010 --> 00:46:41.860
processing some things, so we--

00:46:41.860 --> 00:46:44.360
I don't know if you can read,
but there is something written

00:46:44.360 --> 00:46:48.560
CPU=1 and max backlog=x.

00:46:48.560 --> 00:46:51.140
Let's say like three
images, so by combining

00:46:51.140 --> 00:46:54.080
the two you actually scale
on the size of the Pub-Sub

00:46:54.080 --> 00:46:57.530
and the queue, but
you prevent instances

00:46:57.530 --> 00:46:59.090
that are working to get killed.

00:46:59.090 --> 00:47:01.790
And this is like a
combination of those two

00:47:01.790 --> 00:47:06.150
parameters in your auto scaler
prevent that from happening.

00:47:06.150 --> 00:47:07.730
In the next few
slides we're going

00:47:07.730 --> 00:47:10.400
to look at how Airbus
implemented this.

00:47:10.400 --> 00:47:13.820
So they actually have a
couple of photo IDs as well.

00:47:13.820 --> 00:47:16.230
And I wasn't supposed
to present that,

00:47:16.230 --> 00:47:18.620
but Tomas, which you might
have seen on the first slide,

00:47:18.620 --> 00:47:19.994
actually had a
baby last weekend.

00:47:19.994 --> 00:47:21.800
So [INAUDIBLE].

00:47:21.800 --> 00:47:26.390
And so you're stuck with me
for another three slides.

00:47:26.390 --> 00:47:28.360
So the requirements:
were a lot of the things

00:47:28.360 --> 00:47:30.540
that we already mentioned.

00:47:30.540 --> 00:47:32.960
Airbus needed near real
time processing, which

00:47:32.960 --> 00:47:35.720
means that they don't need
images to be actually treated

00:47:35.720 --> 00:47:38.630
in real time but they need to
be treated as fast as possible.

00:47:38.630 --> 00:47:41.840
So you don't want the
queue to basically wait

00:47:41.840 --> 00:47:44.810
for like three hours because
you only have like two instances

00:47:44.810 --> 00:47:45.310
there.

00:47:45.310 --> 00:47:48.080
So you have to be able to
scale up and down based

00:47:48.080 --> 00:47:51.590
on the size of the queue.

00:47:51.590 --> 00:47:53.540
Another one is like
the jobs are actually

00:47:53.540 --> 00:47:57.260
irregular across the year and
even like across the months,

00:47:57.260 --> 00:47:58.070
across the dates.

00:47:58.070 --> 00:48:00.530
Sometimes like you might
have to do like a mosaic that

00:48:00.530 --> 00:48:03.200
is done yearly,
or you might have

00:48:03.200 --> 00:48:05.090
to kick in like a
job for agriculture

00:48:05.090 --> 00:48:07.170
but you don't really
know when it's happening.

00:48:07.170 --> 00:48:08.810
So when nothing
thing is happening,

00:48:08.810 --> 00:48:11.452
you obviously don't want to
have any instance running

00:48:11.452 --> 00:48:13.160
because that might
last for a few months.

00:48:13.160 --> 00:48:14.900
And when something is
happening you actually

00:48:14.900 --> 00:48:16.608
want to be able to
start those instances.

00:48:16.608 --> 00:48:19.040
So they needed
something to scale down

00:48:19.040 --> 00:48:22.370
to zero, which we
actually mentioned before.

00:48:22.370 --> 00:48:24.320
And they have long
running processes.

00:48:24.320 --> 00:48:26.520
So as we said, like
processing an image

00:48:26.520 --> 00:48:29.240
can take in between 10
minutes and a few hours.

00:48:29.240 --> 00:48:32.600
This is why you don't want to
actually kill an instance that

00:48:32.600 --> 00:48:33.680
is processing something.

00:48:33.680 --> 00:48:36.050
Otherwise while the job is
going to get back into the queue

00:48:36.050 --> 00:48:38.508
and then you're going to have
to wait for another few hours

00:48:38.508 --> 00:48:39.590
to process that image.

00:48:39.590 --> 00:48:42.950
And the last one is what type
of auto scaler do I need?

00:48:42.950 --> 00:48:44.610
We mentioned that earlier.

00:48:44.610 --> 00:48:46.820
So do I want an auto
scaler based on the CPU,

00:48:46.820 --> 00:48:49.310
do I want an auto scaler
based on the custom metrics,

00:48:49.310 --> 00:48:51.884
or do I actually want an auto
scaler based on something else?

00:48:51.884 --> 00:48:53.300
So in that case
sometimes you have

00:48:53.300 --> 00:48:58.370
instances that actually
you are using no CPU,

00:48:58.370 --> 00:48:59.490
but are waiting on IOs.

00:48:59.490 --> 00:49:03.060
So basically most of the
metrics that we had in standards

00:49:03.060 --> 00:49:04.670
didn't work, so
you actually have

00:49:04.670 --> 00:49:07.610
to base that on the amount of
images that are in the queue

00:49:07.610 --> 00:49:09.020
to be able to process.

00:49:09.020 --> 00:49:10.666
So those were all
the requirements,

00:49:10.666 --> 00:49:12.290
and they came up with
a couple of ideas

00:49:12.290 --> 00:49:14.760
or we come up with
a couple of ideas.

00:49:14.760 --> 00:49:17.900
One of them was to actually
use a gatekeeper, which

00:49:17.900 --> 00:49:20.480
is like a small
e-tolling instance

00:49:20.480 --> 00:49:22.880
that we'll look at a queue.

00:49:22.880 --> 00:49:24.810
See when there is a new
image that's coming,

00:49:24.810 --> 00:49:27.260
see if that image should
actually be processed,

00:49:27.260 --> 00:49:32.480
and put that image metadata
into a worker queue.

00:49:32.480 --> 00:49:34.250
And the workers will look at it.

00:49:34.250 --> 00:49:37.000
One is going to pick it up
and say, OK now, I'm working

00:49:37.000 --> 00:49:39.260
and every instance
have metadata.

00:49:39.260 --> 00:49:43.570
So they say I'm active, don't
do anything to me, please.

00:49:43.570 --> 00:49:45.320
In this same time
the gatekeeper will

00:49:45.320 --> 00:49:49.130
out in our data stall, which is
a free manage NoSQL Database.

00:49:49.130 --> 00:49:53.360
We'll say, OK, I have like
an image being processed,

00:49:53.360 --> 00:49:56.690
and this is how I keep track of
how many are being processed.

00:49:56.690 --> 00:49:59.630
And the worker, when they
are done will basically

00:49:59.630 --> 00:50:00.860
say, OK, that's it, I'm done.

00:50:00.860 --> 00:50:03.260
If you need to
kill me please do.

00:50:03.260 --> 00:50:04.460
I uploaded the image.

00:50:04.460 --> 00:50:06.260
And the gatekeeper
will be able to look

00:50:06.260 --> 00:50:11.210
at the ratio of how many
messages are in a queue

00:50:11.210 --> 00:50:12.770
and how many
workers are leaving.

00:50:12.770 --> 00:50:14.480
And then based on
that ratio will

00:50:14.480 --> 00:50:15.710
be able to scale up all them.

00:50:15.710 --> 00:50:18.180
So this was one solution.

00:50:18.180 --> 00:50:22.040
The second solution that I set
up is a little bit simpler.

00:50:22.040 --> 00:50:23.690
So you still have
that worker queue,

00:50:23.690 --> 00:50:26.930
you still have an auto
scale number of workers.

00:50:26.930 --> 00:50:30.300
And then there is like a
Cron is running every minute

00:50:30.300 --> 00:50:35.070
and actually use monitoring
API from Pub/Sub to check, OK,

00:50:35.070 --> 00:50:40.580
how many messages do I
have in the queue that

00:50:40.580 --> 00:50:42.330
haven't been processed yet?

00:50:42.330 --> 00:50:44.616
And then based on
that, and based

00:50:44.616 --> 00:50:45.990
on the work that
are working, I'm

00:50:45.990 --> 00:50:48.115
going to decide whether I
need to scale up or down.

00:50:48.115 --> 00:50:51.690
So it's more like it's not
an event based auto scaler,

00:50:51.690 --> 00:50:54.300
it's more like something that
happens every minute based

00:50:54.300 --> 00:50:58.140
on the statistic that you
get from our monitoring tool.

00:50:58.140 --> 00:51:00.240
So both are working,
they've been

00:51:00.240 --> 00:51:02.509
implemented on
different projects

00:51:02.509 --> 00:51:03.550
depending on their needs.

00:51:03.550 --> 00:51:05.000
So it's really up
to you to see, OK,

00:51:05.000 --> 00:51:06.540
do I need something
right away or do

00:51:06.540 --> 00:51:07.990
I need something every minute.

00:51:07.990 --> 00:51:11.040
It depends on your processes,
or you can also use the Pub/Sub

00:51:11.040 --> 00:51:12.420
as we mentioned earlier.

00:51:15.010 --> 00:51:17.560
This is an example
of what is going on.

00:51:17.560 --> 00:51:20.040
So if we just look at
the top two screens

00:51:20.040 --> 00:51:23.430
you can see on the left
how many jobs are needed,

00:51:23.430 --> 00:51:25.930
so how many images
need to be processed.

00:51:25.930 --> 00:51:27.360
And then you can
see on the right

00:51:27.360 --> 00:51:29.190
how many instances
are actually working.

00:51:29.190 --> 00:51:31.730
So total CPU, if you
see 8 in red percent,

00:51:31.730 --> 00:51:34.135
means there are actually
eight instances working.

00:51:34.135 --> 00:51:35.760
And you can see it's
actually matching,

00:51:35.760 --> 00:51:37.080
like they're both green lines.

00:51:37.080 --> 00:51:41.060
Well, not really lines but a
mountains actually matching.

00:51:41.060 --> 00:51:43.230
So if you look at like 2:00 p.m.

00:51:43.230 --> 00:51:45.750
you can see there is an
increase of how many jobs

00:51:45.750 --> 00:51:46.780
I need to be processed.

00:51:46.780 --> 00:51:49.670
And you can see in the meantime
like the CPU is working

00:51:49.670 --> 00:51:51.600
and more instances are created.

00:51:51.600 --> 00:51:57.690
And if you look at the end,
you don't need any image,

00:51:57.690 --> 00:51:58.680
it's actually at zero.

00:51:58.680 --> 00:52:02.040
And we also have zero instance
on the right over there.

00:52:02.040 --> 00:52:03.620
And if you look at
the worker CPUs,

00:52:03.620 --> 00:52:06.930
so you can look at per worker,
how's the CPU you look like?

00:52:06.930 --> 00:52:10.350
The bottom line is our F1
micro, which was actually

00:52:10.350 --> 00:52:13.920
managing the auto scaler.

00:52:13.920 --> 00:52:16.650
So this is about it for like
explaining a few concept

00:52:16.650 --> 00:52:18.180
for auto scaling.

00:52:18.180 --> 00:52:20.505
Happy to answer a
question after a session.

